#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77c7dfa0-202b-5af6-9228-dc6355dcb12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564151} {:answer/id #uuid "81f383ab-ca0f-54cf-8cd9-153255df4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564168} {:answer/id #uuid "82168b66-7a44-5eb9-8fa3-a04f1522bfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564169} {:answer/id #uuid "ac321221-fe9b-5704-a522-fd0ed1517250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564232} {:answer/id #uuid "c4543906-53e0-543b-8b49-5cef8732311e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564168} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ff271c5-90b5-5fb1-aa9a-8de6b8aed244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "598960e7-33b5-506e-acd3-0cfad9c377b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162231" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48848976-5af9-59a1-a8d5-7f34db74d877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565043} {:answer/id #uuid "77866715-4a00-54a6-9bca-78618431fd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565118} {:answer/id #uuid "984fc114-e07e-535b-bbfd-cf1625fa9db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565168} {:answer/id #uuid "c27ee232-913c-501f-8f38-24c7f5cfaae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565232} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d96702-504b-5f4f-9d1f-1e62ddfecd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c29978b-66bd-5fe3-92c9-25071bfcad0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e84a1e0-ab07-582e-a187-ce57adaa478b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74961" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{1-3+2}{\\sqrt{3-2}-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x-z})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{-(x-z-y)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{3-2}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2776b54f-1b5d-5a24-9264-60188b98a30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565001} {:answer/id #uuid "47a3084a-dea7-5252-a052-f22a8d9afbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565041} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2058cc-d3ee-5a9d-abaa-0c2aab0fc895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565157} {:answer/id #uuid "91e130a9-184e-5357-91fa-a31e71ffed64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565159} {:answer/id #uuid "9b09b8cb-03b3-5495-a712-64af8592a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565001} :answer-field/id #uuid "a63c1194-144a-5227-ac9c-0935b59c5eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "416deefa-43e7-520e-bca5-f8da057711d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74967" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=0$ (the $x$-axis),  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c334b8-5f64-5024-8be8-3440026a135f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564995} {:answer/id #uuid "b3785525-f411-5114-943e-5a2cf050adfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565205} {:answer/id #uuid "b689f50d-16ad-525a-aff0-c88e838473ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565212} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cccb62-ae0c-505e-946a-32fd9dfe1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} {:answer/id #uuid "d4522578-3775-5aa6-9639-2a945876ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1873304-acce-598c-9592-a96cff0a14c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "585a757a-6bf9-546a-9bf9-80289d848f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-74968" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c22d73-cf17-502a-b856-207b42d6d523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565045} {:answer/id #uuid "649ec646-7b0f-548e-9c35-08857d9dbb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565093} {:answer/id #uuid "804fbbe3-5623-55ba-bc08-0b6088ac245d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565135} {:answer/id #uuid "b490877c-e83c-59f4-a1c9-d3fa593a9f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565210} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15ba70-79a7-5e28-8379-b7e2a244dd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565135} :answer-field/id #uuid "eecb02cb-44a7-5766-9e3d-1d6dd4f8028f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6491bac7-604f-53bc-80e6-378575a48cb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129324" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x-y+3.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-2,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3) & =2(-2)-0+3 \\\\  & =-4-0+3 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f47eef-aa78-50a5-b976-e93df5f4c292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564942} {:answer/id #uuid "41b96b77-5e73-5c67-af13-27ac8b7eb13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565034} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a30f22-3124-51ba-913f-5a068a49c15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565237} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bef289-3178-5ae8-94bb-0bc7078744bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565261} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c561d0-62a5-54e4-bf55-93094dadec30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565034} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3985035-b9b2-56db-8d94-fdc34f531158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbddc293-6a3c-5b64-88ba-c447f360dc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129347" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\frac{9-({1}^{2})({3}^{2})}{\\sqrt{9}-1(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-9}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{(\\sqrt{x}-yz)(\\sqrt{x}+yz)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{\\cancel{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz) & =\\sqrt{9}+1(3) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116e62d7-9676-5ce3-b68b-c1b2b15289ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564966} {:answer/id #uuid "1c785494-6256-5978-8b37-733bf1a00a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564980} {:answer/id #uuid "55deb21b-ba9f-58dd-91dc-0aad1e32f9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565065} {:answer/id #uuid "b04bbf06-0a8f-5897-8e52-3272aed01886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565200} {:answer/id #uuid "f3957818-b350-5503-95cc-19f562857757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565306} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ebaa1a-8a6b-5d7a-acd1-3db045338942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec0d423c-ac59-5358-a37f-88f2016b24ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-65138" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy+xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x+y)+y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)+y(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(2x+y)(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y} & =\\frac{2(-1)+(1)}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f286019-c88c-5842-a7b8-9eb547678959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{29}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565613} {:answer/id #uuid "7a58a0e3-980e-505e-83a7-96cf992263a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565615} {:answer/id #uuid "7d16a415-ebbc-506a-8125-0d913b5c4e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565616} {:answer/id #uuid "a3aea7ea-5977-53db-a932-5f809f16357f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565620} {:answer/id #uuid "f48f6b83-2ed6-5e6c-95c1-3d1c22810f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565613} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5254726-4499-591e-9b5e-d6416edfab27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "893b4f39-63b4-53ad-9436-0ea8a59d0fab" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3fa6928e03ea158677a7a95970bd4a5694fecf07e29898395945508ce55c2b69.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/2e/2e7f98a3a56071215be52dcf70f568d868b6314b5e4831828cb6a83a0cc51966.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=5$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5)^2+(2)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25+4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $\\sqrt{29}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{29}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b4b1ab-c04f-5513-81bf-fbc605f7ad3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565612} {:answer/id #uuid "a3928650-7241-534e-8f5e-a62180edd706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565619} {:answer/id #uuid "a4dc3529-b0d3-5899-bceb-b3f38f462306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565621} {:answer/id #uuid "ae52f227-de5a-5674-82ed-9e675e166b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{5}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565622} {:answer/id #uuid "bd729940-7a3a-5abd-852b-38d2fdb1f70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565623}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565612} :answer-field/id #uuid "504d3e27-e309-58e8-a757-b9930747358e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9bfbf2d2-bfbe-5c67-a31b-342360e76488" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d36006c69a9e18e6b603641b4f0ce4f197c5be279b8c8cc4aa3921e3da98c41.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/6b/6b6d4c70f67251eacc9199fd18bb57f7b2ee6a84f24f33851e3f03e3a6796edd.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=2$ and $BH=4$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(4)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4+16} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $2 \\sqrt{5}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a507ce6-22ce-56f0-ba53-527362e98081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x^{3}+16xy^{2}-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565924} :answer-field/id #uuid "5406b9c4-8f08-5b5f-8ea0-96705a9a7215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d7ebf34-a035-5c2e-ad76-841acaa3c221" :question/math-academy-id "q-168886" :question/problem "$\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}) & =2\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})+8{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-8\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+8{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x-8\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11fcb828-059a-5b00-8660-ee47b5e28011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566323} {:answer/id #uuid "74f50dc5-0a42-51fd-aac8-68ff00349587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566330} {:answer/id #uuid "98dd91b7-8d14-56b2-a1e8-0b721a6c8258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566337} {:answer/id #uuid "b5061c58-00d2-5d37-a548-4327c1e9cfff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566340} {:answer/id #uuid "f69386f5-76af-5f63-b1dc-8496e71647c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566340} :answer-field/id #uuid "df243b61-8f96-547c-80a5-1ff5fccc92bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12d71664-9462-5b6b-9acc-39e5ce4b6248" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨-6,\\sqrt{3},-3⟩$ and $d=⟨x,0,4⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $c$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $d$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\nc⊥d\\,⟹\\,c\\cdot d=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot x+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ -6x-12 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5828ae62-3738-52b0-922a-9a4ffb053014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566329} {:answer/id #uuid "807e3ee8-9a19-5f35-ac82-9722a2f30fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566332} {:answer/id #uuid "adef9f69-ed4f-5f90-a2b7-975281838852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566338} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2d33dd-c1cf-5d35-8dd6-3b4cf644c4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566339} {:answer/id #uuid "e67cf0b3-a5f0-5f35-b6f6-9bf7fe8d2c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566329} :answer-field/id #uuid "e11eb96d-e7dc-52c4-9123-2d1bd9ea9fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3136e468-22e2-5b9b-9018-ade50c556fcb" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ x \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot x+(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -24-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beec58e7-394b-543f-b27d-2229014ddea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566341} :answer-field/id #uuid "8495409f-7466-5d7e-bdcf-38abe93e897b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8baa0727-e67d-5a28-8356-6a625710bbcb" :question/problem "Let $p=-3i+4j-k$ and $q=xi+4j.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot x+4\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =16-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 16-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =16 \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3ecdc1-dbf4-5395-80c7-14df65f947bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566348} :answer-field/id #uuid "2630b6a0-813c-5201-8ae0-2e367ed5decb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c7178bb3-73b0-54fb-9b1c-5cefbba69dbe" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ x \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 6+(-6)\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -6x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d29c5f2-2460-5725-85a4-e470e412922d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566326} {:answer/id #uuid "74f564bb-5545-5c3f-8d82-84df0f1a48c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566331} {:answer/id #uuid "84132974-647d-5614-8e2e-1b8f7feafb62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566333} {:answer/id #uuid "91e5e387-9c93-546c-bc9b-7ab7d6cd9b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566336} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a7621d-4c1d-5aed-9d44-1dc3e6f59810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566331} :answer-field/id #uuid "457c83f2-9e57-5302-a582-123b12dda095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d8eeb01b-2308-5126-8d01-0e5889155128" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)+x\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4+4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -4+4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3687686f-27d7-5790-b17c-25679b2d1bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566472} {:answer/id #uuid "98ba232a-4707-557a-b5e6-2e7896a3faba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{x-1}-3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566662} {:answer/id #uuid "b619f3ad-5474-53a1-863a-0d77cd05c096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{x-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566721} {:answer/id #uuid "b787c94d-cc66-56fe-ab2f-bae4827c49ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566727} {:answer/id #uuid "d90c9bfa-7951-5e10-bace-6cd8713f434a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566472} :answer-field/id #uuid "31a1c922-50b2-51e3-a611-69b79808fdf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "059319c8-1dff-5dfe-8695-5074282cdfe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132248" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-{z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0163c90f-63f7-520c-b90b-cdca85bc5861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566364} {:answer/id #uuid "78d2aa47-e366-536d-979b-7aaddb43183d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566591} {:answer/id #uuid "90f591ac-d503-53e4-9edf-df0ecead1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566647} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8aa054-536b-5175-8e3d-21f8ecd92148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566710} {:answer/id #uuid "c62a4f9c-30eb-5f65-9868-b6c9578e4d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6y-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566591} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af82be6-e454-517f-89c6-df6d183337b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1691ed28-0c5c-54a8-9012-9dfc00861f65" :question/math-academy-id "q-159719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(7y-6x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60243795-2a92-591c-983d-ab0a12df51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566544} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccef17a-7e81-5ad2-b543-a2a7ee459876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-y)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566601} {:answer/id #uuid "b662e7db-0fcb-5572-a49f-f705511b1c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566724} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f81ca3-bfba-5c9f-b61e-29a424ecf9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566781} {:answer/id #uuid "e65931e8-e876-5d62-8009-c6a3aab5a425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566544} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55eaa90-3c66-5bf4-a7e7-2ac1f5a2db6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d644ebc-b214-5b17-9daa-3ced5a3257b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159712" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y))\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot 1}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)){e}^{x+y}}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bae9d67-b69f-5f47-b67a-7079c02affc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566388} {:answer/id #uuid "0f14b9cf-9b15-5156-b4dd-56a81035a051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2y{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566392} {:answer/id #uuid "44ca59e7-cf5e-53c0-b7ed-60b5a8af0113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566497} {:answer/id #uuid "8e59a02a-bdaa-55fc-af47-354a579ea6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566638} {:answer/id #uuid "d51bdaab-f954-5323-81d6-75ecb0d92637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566497} :answer-field/id #uuid "809f59c6-6dda-58ee-9a9d-a816868161a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "67e10996-e62d-5489-9a64-73c5e56228d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159710" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x((1)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df86f71-4e8c-5223-a2c3-114b3c3a90ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566425} {:answer/id #uuid "23ed9481-6f88-5b6b-806c-7902c0dee17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566439} {:answer/id #uuid "755654b4-0e0c-54a7-a879-4314f25dc630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566584} {:answer/id #uuid "79a314e6-0668-5c44-b3d1-02a3cf9ad5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566595} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9ccd66-db58-59f7-af47-1c86f73f9c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566439} :answer-field/id #uuid "789cacb9-1990-5456-9318-5cf3591eaa13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "738ade6c-0972-56b4-b1bb-9f8fdeb237e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159661" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (0+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3+1}}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1152fe53-6276-5ced-85b3-1185032862f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x{z}^{2}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566397} {:answer/id #uuid "309e17aa-61ae-54a8-8688-b433b95eac50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566459} {:answer/id #uuid "342562b9-a910-5c9b-8120-d05d2a2c5abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566464} {:answer/id #uuid "ba79e0ef-a3a4-5a04-b1f2-586831978e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566734} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bdccd4-86bb-5a36-b660-24e6958bf711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566464} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69bcd40-ed79-55be-8b7b-cb7c0491af3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a6bdefd5-aef3-5d16-afc3-d28fde40b19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132245" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ for $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4}) & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{4})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (0-x\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{4})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot x\\cdot 4{z}^{3} \\\\  & =-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139d6455-ad86-5abf-8c88-8199b32eebe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566400} {:answer/id #uuid "730f6e85-bd23-53e6-ab08-4141d676fa5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566580} {:answer/id #uuid "a029f170-0196-57bc-a811-b716dfabb716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566674} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ea4bae-10d8-5476-97e9-6ae33a493dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566798} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b3f484-b92d-53c5-8123-dbca1cc7dffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566798} :answer-field/id #uuid "716e7851-801e-59a3-b80e-fe5f30999db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b7285b21-99ee-5b47-b1b3-ef79da9b3c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159698" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) & =(3{x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253445fe-fff1-58b7-9019-3e38491dac58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566442} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddd0cfc-1ccf-5d4e-857f-c5a638f03f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566455} {:answer/id #uuid "72a3ae63-0575-5426-a7c0-15120c576ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566577} {:answer/id #uuid "87430684-ea2a-5175-8c38-072537596c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566619} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a6a36f-25e4-5d5d-aa20-c816606c275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566686} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e6bb6c0-dbeb-5577-8434-7c53a76a2f69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dfe77a47-89cf-58c9-a7e4-db0e7d6a133d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159662" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differenciation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+{y}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f3edd2-28d1-5513-87e8-c881765ba4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i-3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567432} {:answer/id #uuid "99c9a946-38a2-55a3-8063-dbffd3f7fb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567440} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dfeede-c920-57a7-84b3-7ef22805a200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567445} {:answer/id #uuid "e74375bc-1ad7-51fa-973a-ad68c92af852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(3\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567452} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ec0fd9-64f5-5f3c-a3bb-aad8ae20b645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567454} :answer-field/id #uuid "214b114b-d57c-50b7-9ca4-13d6e7035711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "161f4c79-57b1-5774-afbb-2d7aca169df7" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(3,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇g(3,1) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{(3)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{(1)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(3,1)}{‖∇g(3,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285af7d1-a87a-5625-a6b6-d8dcc568f173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567425} {:answer/id #uuid "527d27a2-bb71-572c-8834-d84b72906ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i-3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567434} {:answer/id #uuid "626368b7-98b7-50d4-9eb8-f70103c98cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567436} {:answer/id #uuid "91db4913-a017-59b5-8d28-26fcc2fa2d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567439} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3ec1b9-2e36-56c3-a2f7-cbd42322dc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(3\\,i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567439} :answer-field/id #uuid "34900a04-64dc-5527-90bc-fb1ee330dcb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33daf655-8020-5d37-af88-f1b07a868f33" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(9,16).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(9,16)$ can be found by computing $∇f(9,16).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(9,16) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(9,16)}{‖∇f(9,16)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)}{‖\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa7421b-25b5-54d9-abc5-b3f30bf4e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(-5\\,i+13\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567426} {:answer/id #uuid "377104d7-3a96-5797-8a46-52faa5fe3930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i-5\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567429} {:answer/id #uuid "4152bee6-a103-5066-9916-e8ec290e17bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567431} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a8d96b-d251-5b81-816c-277b51ae94ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{18}\\,i-\\frac{5}{18}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567446} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b431a5-1b4e-55e9-be8b-e4d0913658c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i+5\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567429} :answer-field/id #uuid "280e4d68-ea50-53bf-b04a-a692f502867f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a3e908d9-bc89-55b4-a12b-976025ac1c38" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5)$, can be found by computing $∇f(13,5).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(13,5) & =\\left(\\frac{(13)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(-\\frac{(5)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,i-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(13,5)}{‖∇f(13,5)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{13}{12}\\right)^{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{12}\\right)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i-5\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061cb6cc-3959-5b14-a981-3106b485c5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567421} {:answer/id #uuid "3e51dd0e-8bba-5b42-b988-422251d36d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567430} {:answer/id #uuid "58e6f7ff-07ca-5404-a2f8-04a18016cc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567435} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9ab139-6632-5ccc-9f6f-ba0573cb2c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567441} {:answer/id #uuid "a337bb17-7b8c-5901-9a49-487470e0c389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567421} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc9e5b3-fcfc-594a-bbf3-8c741fb6bf4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b488d0d8-0d5c-58db-a90e-48c42f1d42c6" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =\\left(\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(-\\frac{(0)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,i-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,j \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,0)}{‖∇f(1,0)‖} & =\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{i}{1} \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516067a1-6bca-5069-bbab-140935f51b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567433} {:answer/id #uuid "86eaf185-0d45-569a-a7a0-8dff4d65f888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567437} {:answer/id #uuid "8e50f7c7-1827-5994-b8f2-c35e30b7dddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567438} {:answer/id #uuid "9d56dc45-fddc-52b7-8158-e2e3313891b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567442} {:answer/id #uuid "d514d19a-83e2-56dc-ae0c-76ff2bae7b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567442} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4faeac1-9e8b-5c11-aec7-607387173f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db955fef-1148-5a1c-a192-f4c8263ee71a" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(4,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(4,0) & =\\left(-\\frac{(0)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(\\frac{(4)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{16}\\,i+\\frac{4}{16}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(4,0)}{‖∇f(4,0)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(j)}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}(j) \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109532ae-3aee-59db-8bde-7e9cba85b118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567494} {:answer/id #uuid "a396f3e0-9c87-5fe1-b58b-8d2355bd28e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=i-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567515} {:answer/id #uuid "d2507728-6208-59a6-a680-8f31d72c300e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=z\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567522} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b5ef09-1881-5165-8249-d93360c6b201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567523} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7c8468-6f18-5421-a9bc-0963711063f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567525} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca374de3-039a-52f1-af9c-7f8e3070828f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "31b513e6-9d42-5737-bc07-fdcb248e5ca9" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fec3ce0d1886d6ee9a568aad2fab68039e282b2bd93c69196628731c791854e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $k$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=i-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=z\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=2\\,i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102f7b24-3534-5164-b723-728cdc381690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-2\\,z\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567493} {:answer/id #uuid "5b10465a-21c9-5c3d-939d-12d6a0f846cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567501} {:answer/id #uuid "5f242240-f9c5-5b71-bf0e-d327e5405f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567502} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ec15f5-2848-5946-81a6-00d2bcad267d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-4\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567517} {:answer/id #uuid "fddeb8cd-88ae-57ad-bc6e-0a98f108f03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567517} :answer-field/id #uuid "8835ac58-e3ea-529a-9813-3f809795e2fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83f13c2f-1a04-5982-a206-6fd3fbd8ffbf" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f5e69364a876f0714abedacd9e8cecd282826318e12408e8943b1e824dcde09.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $k$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-2\\,i+2\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2\\,i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-2\\,z\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-4\\,i.$"}]}
